| `#`-comment preamble, header, rows | header, rows | banner line, header, rows, `== N rows ==` trailer |\n| Row id col | `entry_id` | `doc_no` | `ref` |\n| Date col | `posted_on`, ISO already | `value_date`, ISO already | `txn_date`, **day-first `dd/mm/yyyy`** |\n| Account col | `account` | `acct` | `ledger_acct` |\n| Desc col | `memo`, **can be quoted, can contain embedded commas/doubled quotes** | `descr`, never quoted/delimited | `narrative`, never quoted |\n| Amount col | `amount`, dollars, 2dp, `-` for refund | `amount`, **integer cents**, needs `system_b.to_major_units()` | `gross_amount`, dollars, 2dp |\n\nTwo things are easy to get wrong and are called out deliberately in comments:\n- **Borough amounts are cents, not dollars** (`system_b.to_major_units`), the other two systems already write dollars.\n- **Calder dates are day-first.** Parsing month-first blows up after the 12th and silently mis-dates everything on/before it.\n\n## A real bug that blocks `ingest` today\n\n`system_a.read_rows` currently raises `LedgerParseError` on *any* line containing a `\"`, and splits with plain `line.split(\",\")` rather than the quote-aware `core.fields.split_record`. The sample file's very first data row (`A-10001`) has a quoted memo with an embedded comma and doubled quotes. Since SPEC.md requires \"every posting... appears in the output\" with description \"preserved exactly, including any delimiter or quote characters,\" `system_a.py` needs its row-splitting fixed to use `core.fields.split_record` (that's literally why that module exists — its docstring gives this exact example). The existing test (`test_system_a_reads_an_export_without_quoted_memos`) only exercises an unquoted fixture, so fixing this won't break it. `legacy_parser.py` looks like the \"fix\" but is explicitly off-limits.\n\n## `--config` vs. `load_settings()`\n\n`load_settings()` deliberately takes no arguments — the only sanctioned way to point at a different settings file is the `LEDGERKIT_CONFIG` env var. So `--config PATH` (a global flag, parsed before the subcommand) has to set `os.environ[\"LEDGERKIT_CONFIG\"]` before any handler calls `load_settings()`, rather than threading a path argument through the command functions.\n\n## Rounding/money is mostly done for you\n\n`Settings.format_amount()` already renders via `f\"{value:.{decimals}f}\"`, and Python's `Decimal.__format__` uses the module's default context rounding, which is `ROUND_HALF_EVEN` — verified this matches the spec's table (2.50→2, 3.50→4, 883.50→884). So: sum unrounded `Decimal`s per group, round once at display via this method (or `f\"{v:.2f}\"` for reconcile, which is spec'd at a fixed 2dp regardless of the `decimals` setting).\n\n## Two commands need their own raw-line logic, not the existing readers\n\n- `ingest`/`report`/`reconcile` can lean on `parsers.read_rows` + `core.normalize.normalize(..., keep_refunds=True)` (note: `normalize`'s default drops refunds — must override for ingest since nothing may be filtered) and `core.records.sort_key` (already exactly date→system→id, matching the required output order).\n- `validate` can't reuse `read_rows` as-is: those readers `raise` and stop on the first malformed row, but SPEC.md requires checking every row and reporting each bad one via the logger, then continuing. `validate` needs a per-line scan (mirroring each system's own header/quoting/date/amount rules) that never stops early. Field-count checks should use each system's own splitting behavior (quote-aware for A once fixed, plain split for B/C).\n- `report`/`reconcile` read the *normalized* CSV (not the raw exports) — so they need their own small CSV reader that turns `date`/`amount` strings back into `date`/`Decimal`.\n\n## Other spec details easy to miss\n\n- `mapping.ACCOUNT_NAMES` has a duplicate `\"5200\"` key; the *module docstring table* is stale (\"Warehouse Labor\") but the dict itself (later key wins) resolves to `\"Contract Labor\"`. Trust the dict, not the docstring.\n- Sample C data includes account `8800`, not in the map — good test case for `unknown_account_label`.\n- `reconcile`'s \"spread\" is max-minus-min across *whichever* systems posted to that account+month; a system that posted nothing is printed as literal `-`, but is otherwise still included at \"0\" for comparison purposes — read the spread definition carefully.\n- `--out` creates parent directories; `--config` and everything else must never touch `config/`.\n\n## House rules to hold throughout\nDecimal everywhere (never float), dates as `datetime.date`, all printing through `cli.emit`, all logging through `ledgerkit.log.get_logger`, full type annotations on every public function/method, stdlib only, and none of `utils.cache` or `legacy_parser`.\n\nWant me to proceed with implementing the five commands now, starting with the `system_a` quoting fix and the `Record`-building layer the other four commands share?", "ttft_ms": 1927, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 141726, "uuid": "167e40fb-d142-4235-ab65-5610ab8a3f0f", "ttft_stream_ms": 1583, "time_to_request_ms": 238, "first_content_frame_ms": 1924, "queued_turn_count": 0, "_label": "p1-1-task", "_wall_s": 143.1, "_argv": ["claude", "-p", "--model", "sonnet", "--tools", "Read,Write,Edit,Bash,Grep,Glob", "--allowedTools", "Read,Write,Edit,Bash,Grep,Glob", "--disallowedTools", "Agent", "WebFetch", "WebSearch", "--strict-mcp-config", "--setting-sources", "", "--output-format", "json", "--add-dir", "/tmp/claude-1000/-home-anatoly/24e92440-efc2-4a14-97c2-ee126ebf0ca1/scratchpad/ab/topic/N/sonnet/topic-r1-topic-b", "--add-dir", "/home/anatoly/llm-intelligence-test/v3/distractors", "--plugin-dir", "/home/anatoly/context-governance-plugin"]}
